## 2 · Purpose & problem statement

Deno's CLI drops information that command-line applications need, most acutely on Windows. `dxx` exists to recover that information and to make scripts behave like well-mannered native commands.

The concrete upstream gaps this project works around:

| Gap                                                                            | Upstream ref                                                         |
| ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Scripts cannot learn their own invocation text (`$0` / `%0`)                   | [denoland/deno#9874](https://github.com/denoland/deno/issues/9874)   |
| No access to the raw/original command line (quote context lost in `Deno.args`) | [denoland/deno#9871](https://github.com/denoland/deno/issues/9871)   |
| `CTRL-C` on a shimmed script triggers `Terminate batch job (Y/N)?`             | [denoland/deno#9873](https://github.com/denoland/deno/issues/9873)   |
| `^C^C` echo cannot be suppressed without raw input / signal handling           | [denoland/deno#9995](https://github.com/denoland/deno/issues/9995)   |
| Deno v1.13 broke shims using `--allow-plugin`                                  | [denoland/deno#11819](https://github.com/denoland/deno/issues/11819) |
| `cmd` argument quoting through `Deno.run`                                      | [denoland/deno#8852](https://github.com/denoland/deno/issues/8852)   |

Derived requirements:

- **R1** — On Windows, a target script MUST be able to receive `bash`-like expanded arguments (brace, tilde, glob) **while preserving quote semantics** (`'*'`, `*`, and `"*"` MUST remain distinguishable).
- **R2** — A target script MUST be able to recover the text that invoked it, so that generated help/usage/examples name the real command.
- **R3** — Interrupting a shimmed script MUST NOT leave a `Terminate batch job (Y/N)?` prompt, and the script's exit status MUST reach the parent shell.
- **R4** — Every enhancement MUST degrade gracefully: when enhanced data is unavailable, behavior falls back to plain `Deno.args` and the process MAY warn that it is running _impaired_ (§7).
- **R5** — A script using the library MUST remain compilable to a fully self-contained binary (`deno compile`) with no loss of function.

### Non-goals

- Replacing `deno` itself, or wrapping subcommands beyond those specified here.
- Emulating a full shell (no command substitution, pipelines, redirection, or backslash escapes — see §6).
- POSIX argument enhancement: a modern shell already expands correctly, so POSIX behavior is deliberately pass-through.

