## 5 · Meta-shim protocol (normative)

A **shim** is a transparent adaption layer. A **meta-shim** (or meta-runner, e.g. `dxr`) additionally passes process information to its target through environment variables.

| Variable      | Direction | Meaning                                                                                                      |
| ------------- | --------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `SHIM_TARGET` | in        | path/URL of the script the shim is launching — the **targeting gate** (§4.5)                                 |
| `SHIM_ARG0`   | in        | original `argv[0]`/invocation text                                                                           |
| `SHIM_ARGS`   | in        | original argument text (re-quoted; ≤ 8 KiB)                                                                  |
| `SHIM_PIPE`   | in/out    | path of a generated script the target MAY write to, to push environment/CWD changes back to the parent shell |
| `SHIM_EXEC`   | in        | secondary-shim executable path (implementation detail; Windows)                                              |

- Legacy aliases MUST continue to be read: `SHIM_URL`, `SHIM_ARGV0`, `SHIM_ARGV`, and the `DENO_SHIM_*` forms of all names. New code MUST emit only the primary names.
- Consumers MUST delete every recognized variable after reading (§4.5).
- Oversized argument text MUST be rejected rather than silently truncated. (A file-based transport — `SHIM_ARGF` — is a documented future option.)

### Windows CMD meta-shim requirements

The generated `.cmd` shim (`cmdShimTemplate`) MUST:

1. Capture `%*` into `SHIM_ARGS` with `)` escaped/unescaped around the parse-group boundary, and set `SHIM_ARG0` from `%~0`.
2. Use the `(goto) 2>NUL` exit-the-batch-context trick so that `CTRL-C` does not produce `Terminate batch job (Y/N)?` (R3).
3. Propagate the target's exit code via `SHIM_ERRORLEVEL` and a final `"%COMSPEC%" /d/c "@exit <code>"`, resetting `ERRORLEVEL`/`SHIM_ERRORLEVEL` to avoid pinned values.
4. Set `DENO_NO_UPDATE_CHECK` and `DENO_NO_DEPRECATION_WARNINGS`, and restore their prior values afterwards. It MUST NOT force `DENO_NO_PROMPT` (user choice).
5. When the pipe feature is enabled, build a collision-resistant `SHIM_TID` from `%DATE%`/`%TIME%`/`%RANDOM%` with locale-introduced path-illegal characters removed, then create/consume/delete `SHIM_PIPE`.
6. Honor `SHIM_DEBUG`: when defined, emit diagnostics and retain the pipe file.
7. Self-identify in its first line (`` `<name>` (*enhanced*, Deno CMD meta-shim; by <appNameVersion>) ``) so `shimInfo()` can recognize it later.

_[status]_ Terminology is mid-migration from "enhanced shim" to "meta-shim". New text MUST use **meta-shim**; the template banner and older comments still say "enhanced".

## 6 · Capability model

A process is **enhanced** along two independent axes, and `xProcess` MUST report both:

| Capability               | Satisfied by                                                                                                  |
| ------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `haveSuppliedArgv0` (R2) | shim `ARG0`, a parsed command-line runner, or direct execution (compiled binary)                              |
| `haveEnhancedArgs` (R1)  | being a shim target, shim-supplied script args, an available raw command line, or running under a `*sh` shell |

`impaired` MUST be computed as `!(haveEnhancedArgs && haveSuppliedArgv0)` on Windows, and `!haveSuppliedArgv0` elsewhere. When impaired, `warnIfImpaired()` MUST name which resolution is faulty and MUST point the user at the remedy (`dxr`, or install with `dxi`). Commands choose whether to warn: `dxf` warns; `dxi` deliberately does not (it is commonly run via plain `deno install`).

Three supported routes to full function, which MUST all remain viable:

1. run through a meta-runner (`dxr`),
2. run a command installed with `dxi` (meta-shim),
3. FFI access to the raw command line (`--unstable`/`--unstable-ffi` on older Deno; unflagged on Deno 2+), including inside `deno compile` output.

## 7 · CLI conventions (all `dx*` commands)

- Permissions MUST be declared up front via `abortIfMissingPermitsSync(…)`, with an inline comment stating **why** each permit is needed. Commands MUST fail with a clear message rather than trigger Deno's permission prompt.
- Argument parsing MUST use the project's vendored `yargs` fork with: `camel-case-expansion`, `strip-aliased`, `strip-dashed`; numbers unparsed where the command passes arguments through; `unknown-options-as-args` for the delegating commands. Because `halt-at-non-option` is unreliable alongside `unknown-options-as-args`, commands MUST split `bakedArgs` manually at the first non-option or `--`.
- Arguments MUST come from `$me.args()` (expanded), never `Deno.args` directly.
- All diagnostics go to **STDERR** via the suspended-then-resumed `logger`. Level MUST be resolvable from `LOG_LEVEL`/`LOGLEVEL`/`DEBUG` and from `--log-level`, `--silent`, `--quiet`, `--verbose`, `--debug`, `--trace`; an explicit `--log-level` wins, otherwise the most verbose request wins. Default level: `notice`.
- Color/Unicode use MUST be gated on `mightUseColor()` / `mightUseUnicode()` (honoring `NO_COLOR`, `NO_UNICODE`).
- Help text MUST be rendered via `restyleYargsHelp()` with `yargs`' own wrapping disabled, and MUST include version, usage, grouped options (`Options:`, `*Logging:`, `*Help/Info:`), and copyright.
- A parse failure MUST print the error plus a "use `<cmd> --help` …" hint, and MUST exit `1`.

_[status]_ `--help`/`--version` exit status is **inconsistent**: `dxf` and `dxr` always exit `1`; `dxi` exits `0` when help/version is the only argument and `1` otherwise. `dxi`'s rule is the intended one (help on request is success; help forced by a malformed command line is not). `dxf` and `dxr` SHOULD adopt it.

## 8 · Platform & version compatibility

- Code MUST stay cross-platform. OS-specific behavior MUST be gated on `isWinOS` and the project's path helpers; Windows is the primary target and the only platform where argument enhancement applies.
- **MSDV** (Minimum Supported Deno Version):
  - `std@0.134.0` pins the floor at **Deno 1.20.5**.
  - **Deno 1.23.0+** when synchronous permission queries are avoided (`abortIfMissingPermits(…)`).
  - **Deno 1.30.0+** to use the synchronous permit API (`abortIfMissingPermitsSync(…)`), which is what the commands do — so the effective floor is 1.30.0.
- FFI flag matrix (also encoded in `tests/xArgs.integration.test.ts`):

| Deno range  | Flags for raw-command-line FFI             |
| ----------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| 2.0+        | `--allow-ffi` (or `--allow-all`, not both) |
| 1.40 – 1.46 | `--unstable-ffi` + `--allow-ffi`           |
| 1.38 – 1.39 | `--unstable` or `--unstable-ffi` + allow   |
| 1.13 – 1.37 | `--unstable` + `--allow-ffi`               |

- Version-specific workarounds MUST be gated by `$semver` comparison and MUST carry a comment naming the Deno version and the issue. Currently tracked: `deno install` quiet-output bug (1.29+), `--global` default change (1.42+), `--config` warning (2.1.8+), `--`-before-`COMMAND` requirement (2.6+), and a `--` handling regression in **Deno 2.9.6** ([denoland/deno#36792](https://github.com/denoland/deno/issues/36792)).
- Deprecated `Deno.run` usage MUST be funnelled through `src/lib/$deprecated.ts` rather than called directly, so the migration to `Deno.Command` can proceed module by module.

## 9 · Dependency & vendoring policy

- No `package.json`, no `node_modules`, no lockfile (`"lock": false` — this is deliberate; do not re-enable).
- Third-party and `std` imports MUST be declared **only** in a `$deps*.ts` module and consumed from there. Pins in force: `std@0.134.0` (later versions prompt for permissions), `cliffy@v0.23.0` (last version on `std` ≤ 0.134.0), a project fork of `yargs`, plus `braces`/`picomatch`/`lodash` via jsDelivr ESM.
- Every URL import MUST pin an exact version or commit.
- `vendor/` holds `deno vendor` output (`vendor/deno@<ver>-vendor`) plus a `.patch` overlay applied by [`../tools/rebuild-vendor.BAT`](../tools/rebuild-vendor.BAT). `vendor/` is excluded from `deno`, `dprint`, and the test gates, and MUST NOT be hand-edited.
- Types that upstream ships incorrectly MAY be vendored under `vendor/@types/…` and referenced with `import type`.

## 10 · Quality invariants (enforced)

These are machine-checked; `deno test -A` is the gate.

**Library hygiene** — `tests/00.project.syntax.test.ts`

- Every module under `src/lib/**` MUST be **no-panic**: `deno run --no-prompt <file>` succeeds with zero permissions.
- Every such module MUST be **no-prompt**: importing it produces no permission prompt (checked via `expect`, when available).
- All code files (source, examples, tests, benches, tools) MUST compile with **no warnings** under `deno test --reload --no-run`.
- No circular dependencies (`madge`; currently skipped for `madge` >= 7 pending upstream TypeScript support).

**Style** — `tests/00.project.style.test.ts`

- `deno lint`, `dprint check`, `cspell`, `bmp --info`, and `commitlint` MUST all pass.
- Non-binary project files MUST: contain no leading UTF-8 BOM, end with a newline when non-empty, use **LF** newlines (except `.bat`/`.cmd`/`.sln`/`.vcproj`/`.vcxproj`), and contain no trailing whitespace.
- Excluded from scanning: `*build`, `*coverage`, `fixtures`, `.git`, `.gpg`, `vendor`.

**Packaging & version** — `tests/00.project.package.test.ts`, `tests/00.project.version.test.ts`

- `README`, `LICENSE`, and `CHANGELOG` MUST be present and non-empty.
- `VERSION`, `$shared.ts`'s `VERSION`, and every versioned `deno.land/x/dxx@…` URL in the README MUST agree; on a version-tagged commit, `git describe` MUST agree too.

**Release strictness** — `deno test -A -- --release` (or `--test-dist`/`--test-release`)

- `commitlint --strict`; `wip`/`x`/`version` types and `fixup!`/`squash!` messages are rejected. Flags after `--` set `TEST_<FLAG>=true` via `setEnvFromArgs`.

_Caveat_: console-size/TTY integration tests cannot obtain a real TTY under CI and are special-cased; failures there are usually environmental.

## 11 · Versioning, release & distribution

- Semantic versioning, currently `0.x` — breaking changes are possible between patch releases, and MUST still be marked `!` + `BREAKING CHANGE:` per [`spec-commits.mkd`](spec-commits.mkd).
- `bmp` ([`../.bmp.yml`](../.bmp.yml)) is the single source of version propagation: `VERSION`, `src/lib/$shared.ts`, README URLs, `eg/WanCopy.ts`. Version strings MUST NOT be edited by hand.
- `CHANGELOG.mkd` is generated by `git-changelog` from commit messages and MUST NOT be hand-edited. Commit types therefore determine the changelog; see `spec-commits.mkd`.
- Release sequence: `bmp --[major|minor|patch]` ⇒ `git-changelog --next-tag "v<VERSION>"` ⇒ commit (message = bare version) ⇒ clean tree ⇒ `git tag -f v<VERSION>` ⇒ `deno test -A -- --release` ⇒ push tags.
- Publication is by tag push (`deno.land/x` registry hook); the jsDelivr CDN path additionally supports version-range/latest/commit references.

## 12 · CI/CD

[`../.github/workflows/CICD.yml`](../.github/workflows/CICD.yml), on every push and pull request:

- **Style/Syntax** (ubuntu) — installs `expect`, `bmp`, `commitlint@17`, `cspell@8`, `dprint@0.47.2`, `madge@6`; runs `deno cache` per entry point (converting warnings into GitHub annotations); then the style + syntax gates.
- **Build** (ubuntu · macOS · windows) — full `deno test --allow-all --coverage`; extra TTY-wrapped console-size runs on ubuntu/macOS via `script`/`expect` (unavailable on Windows runners); LCOV upload to CodeCov; `deno compile --allow-all --unstable` of each `src/*.ts` entry point **from the CDN URL** for the current commit (preserving `import.meta.url` project context); packaged as `.zip`/`.tar.gz` artifacts with README + LICENSE.
- **Publish** — on version-like tags only, attaching per-target archives to a GitHub release.

Entry points are discovered by glob (`./src/[^$]*.ts`, `./src/bin/*.ts`, `./src/cli/*.ts`), so a new command file is picked up automatically — and `$`-prefixed modules are excluded by design.

## 13 · Roadmap / known gaps

Ordered roughly by how much they block the documented feature set:

1. **`dxx`** — implement the integrator, or remove it from README/structure docs until it exists (§4.4).
2. **`dxf` fallback** — reconcile code and README (§4.1).
3. **`dxr` permissions** — declare `run` (§4.3).
4. **`--help` exit status** — unify on `dxi`'s rule (§7).
5. **`dxi` shim revision** — upgrade pre-existing shims in place; promote the [`../tools/`](../tools/) prototypes.
6. **`dxr` enhancements** — auto-set requested permissions (warning when inferred), run remote scripts `dpx`-style with extra search directories (`src`, …), and `--install` a remote script.
7. **dexter/xtr** — the file format and its auto-execution are placeholders with no specification yet; they MUST be specified before implementation.
8. **`SHIM_ARGF`** — file-based transport for argument text beyond the environment-block cap (§5).
9. **POSIX meta-shim** — decide the fate of `PosixShimTemplate` (§4.2).
10. **`Deno.run` migration** — retire `$deprecated.ts` usage in favor of `Deno.Command`.
11. **`xWait` ⇒ `rivy/deno.progress`**.
12. **Deno 2.9.6 `--` regression** — tests are currently adjusted around it; re-tighten once upstream is fixed.

## 14 · Glossary

| Term        | Meaning                                                                                |
| ----------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| shim        | adaption layer launching a script; normally transparent                                |
| meta-shim   | shim that additionally passes process info to its target via `SHIM_*` (§5)             |
| meta-runner | runner doing the same without installing anything (`dxr`)                              |
| impaired    | running without reliable `$0` and/or argument resolution (§6)                          |
| MSDV        | Minimum Supported Deno Version (§8)                                                    |
| standalone  | `deno compile` output; executes directly rather than via a runner                      |
| `nullglob`  | a non-matching glob expands to nothing instead of staying literal (§4.6)               |
| TID         | TargetID; per-execution unique suffix for shim pipe files (§5)                         |
| `$`-prefix  | repo convention marking a shared/internal module (`$deps.ts`, `$shared.ts`, `$mod.ts`) |
